WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,525
Permissions describe allowed actions on resources.

2
00:00:03,525 --> 00:00:13,629
A permission might allow reading an object, starting a virtual machine, listing secrets, changing a firewall rule, creating a key, or updating a policy.

3
00:00:13,629 --> 00:00:18,486
Policies are the rules that grant, deny, or condition those permissions.

4
00:00:18,486 --> 00:00:29,139
They may be attached to an identity, a resource, a group, a role, an organization, a folder, a subscription, a project, or another scope depending on the provider.

5
00:00:29,139 --> 00:00:32,272
Cloud environments use several access models.

6
00:00:32,272 --> 00:00:38,382
Role-based access control, or RBAC, grants roles that bundle permissions for a job or scope.

7
00:00:38,382 --> 00:00:50,053
Attribute-based access control, or ABAC, uses attributes such as tags, labels, departments, environments, resource names, or identity properties to make access decisions.

8
00:00:50,053 --> 00:00:53,578
Identity-based policies travel with the principal.

9
00:00:53,578 --> 00:00:57,495
Resource-based policies define who can access a resource.

10
00:00:57,495 --> 00:01:01,176
Conditions can limit when, where, or how access is used.

11
00:01:01,176 --> 00:01:03,918
Explicit deny rules can override grants.

12
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:10,262
Effective access is the access a principal actually has after all relevant rules are evaluated.

13
00:01:10,262 --> 00:01:14,727
It can be different from what one visible role assignment suggests.

14
00:01:14,727 --> 00:01:23,187
A user may inherit access from a group, receive additional access through a role assumption path, be blocked by an explicit deny,

15
00:01:23,187 --> 00:01:25,928
or gain access through a resource policy.

16
00:01:25,928 --> 00:01:33,057
A workload may look limited in one project but have a token that can impersonate a service account elsewhere.

17
00:01:33,057 --> 00:01:36,895
This is why IAM review must look beyond the first role name.

18
00:01:36,895 --> 00:01:40,106
Practical IAM analysis asks several questions.

19
00:01:40,106 --> 00:01:42,613
What principal is requesting access?

20
00:01:42,613 --> 00:01:44,336
What action is requested?

21
00:01:44,336 --> 00:01:46,216
Which resource is affected?

22
00:01:46,216 --> 00:01:50,759
What policies apply from identity, resource, and inherited scopes?

23
00:01:50,759 --> 00:01:52,248
Are there conditions?

24
00:01:52,248 --> 00:01:53,971
Is there an explicit deny?

25
00:01:53,971 --> 00:01:56,164
Is access temporary or standing?

26
00:01:56,164 --> 00:01:59,767
Does another path allow impersonation or delegation?

27
00:01:59,767 --> 00:02:07,914
Understanding effective access is essential for least privilege, troubleshooting, incident response, and governance.

28
00:02:07,914 --> 00:02:10,968
Policy evaluation can also cross boundaries.

29
00:02:10,968 --> 00:02:20,995
A project-level grant, subscription-level role, organization policy, bucket policy, key policy, or service control may all affect the final answer.

30
00:02:20,995 --> 00:02:26,400
Troubleshooting access often means tracing the request through several layers.

31
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,906
Governance review works the same way.

32
00:02:28,906 --> 00:02:35,643
A visible assignment may look safe until inherited permissions or a resource policy adds a wider path.

33
00:02:35,643 --> 00:02:37,992
The practical outcome is humility.

34
00:02:37,992 --> 00:02:42,927
Do not assume a principal has only the permissions you remember assigning.

35
00:02:42,927 --> 00:02:48,959
Use provider tools, access analysis, logs, and policy review to understand actual access.

36
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:54,364
Effective access is what matters during an incident, an audit, or a design review.

37
00:02:54,364 --> 00:02:59,533
It is also what matters when you decide whether a role is truly least privilege.

38
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:03,058
Access design should also account for failure modes.

39
00:03:03,058 --> 00:03:06,661
If a condition depends on a tag, who can change that tag?

40
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:10,735
If access depends on group membership, who manages the group?

41
00:03:10,735 --> 00:03:17,079
If a resource policy grants access to another account or tenant, who reviews that relationship?

42
00:03:17,079 --> 00:03:22,249
Effective access includes both the grant and the governance around the grant.
